#3929: pre-compile before a fork only for scans of 500+ files (small scans keep #3923's 30% win) (#3939)
* #3929: pre-compile before a fork only for a scan of 500+ files

Three-way A/B (pre-#3914 / main / fix, A-vs-A control, whole-command time):
pre-compiling every claimant in the parent took the 3,280-file crucible
corpus to -0.7% (main +0.2%), but made a 40-file C scan slower than main
(-21% vs -26% against pre-#3914): its 3 workers start on idle cores, where
compiling lazily -- in parallel, only what each meets (never objective-c
there) -- beats one serial compile. Large scans keep every core busy to the
end, where a worker's own compile is wall time (#3929: curl -0.47 s).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01X9PBcL8evG7CEzff1zE8v5

* #3914: ReDoS harnesses send their child a plain compiled pattern

assert_redos_immune spawns a child and allows it timeout_sec (1 s) from
start(). Since #3923 a registry rule is a LazyPattern, which unpickles
through gitgalaxy's language registry: the child imported 74 gitgalaxy
modules (258 ms here, more on a loaded runner) inside that window and a
linear rule read as hung (macOS 3.9 on #3939: agc_assembly func_start).

_plain() compiles the rule in the parent; a plain pattern unpickles through
`re` alone (0.6 ms, no gitgalaxy import). _best_of_timing compiles before
its clock too. A disabled rule (None) passes through as before.

